#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697 :topic/difficulty 0.7855885097363628 :topic/id #uuid "b5d86240-2aa3-5be2-b9a5-9ed16336f711" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068447 :question/id #uuid "d1de6657-82b1-5bcb-b8cc-fd2629d31faa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10832" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' = \\dfrac {x^2}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y^2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + C.\n$$\n\nWe can express the solution in the form $y = y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = 2C.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f0876a-33f0-5c6b-9195-8b9a0a550655" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068448 :question/id #uuid "23d66cc8-8e5a-5a33-a7a1-c43f5facdcbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10833" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = e^C$  and $K > 0.$ \n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ke^{-x^2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b784e1-ad0e-5cf2-8b02-604a7098c6a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068449 :question/id #uuid "32040154-373e-5a10-a460-1d17f6989da0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10849" :question/problem "Given that $x,y \\geq 0,$ separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2xy}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  \\sqrt{2x} \\cdot  \\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2x}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326" :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068451 :question/id #uuid "d53c6172-978d-57c7-b1f0-e56e892e48a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10997" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    \\dfrac{x}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C= 2K.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ny^2 = x^2 + C .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67b149c-f701-5be0-868d-c2f4a4be4be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"}] :topic/math-academy-id 466 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050995}] :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Separation of Variables"}]}
